From: paulneja <207793167+paulneja@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 67/67] esp32s3: the panel becomes a console

The panel has been a framebuffer since patch 0063 and nothing more: a
program could open /dev/fb0 and draw, but the kernel had no way to put text
on it, so `echo` and `cat` went to the serial port and the screen stayed
blank. This adds console=tty1 to the command line, which is the half of that
the device tree owns.

The other half is the kernel config -- VT, VT_CONSOLE, FRAMEBUFFER_CONSOLE,
FONTS and FONT_6x8 -- and it is not in this patch because it lives in
nano_esp32_linux.config, where the rest of this board's config lives.

The serial console is not replaced, and the order of the two arguments is
what keeps it that way. `console` is a kernel alias for the *last* console=
on the command line, and /dev/console is routed to the same one. Putting
tty1 after ttyS0 would silently re-point both at the panel: the kernel's own
`/dev/console` would become the screen, and buildroot's inittab line --
`console::respawn:/sbin/getty -L console 0 vt100`, which names the alias
rather than a device -- would move the serial getty onto tty1 with it. The
board then had two gettys on the panel and none on the serial port, so the
serial console took no input at all. ttyS0 is written last so the alias
still means the serial port, which is the default this image is built
around: the panel is an addition to the console, never a replacement for it.

The kernel prints to every console it is given regardless of order, so the
boot log still comes out of UART0 while the same text appears on the panel.

tty1 rather than tty0 because tty0 is an alias for whichever virtual
terminal is currently active, and there is exactly one here. Naming it
directly means the console does not follow a VT switch that nothing on this
board can perform anyway.

The panel's own console binding needs no argument. fbcon maps every virtual
terminal to the first registered framebuffer in fbcon_init, and on xtensa
video_is_primary_device() is the asm-generic stub that returns false, so the
mapping comes from con2fb_map_boot[] being zero-initialised rather than from
the primary-device path. fbcon=map:0 would set the same thing explicitly and
is deliberately not written down: it is a second place to keep true.

One thing to watch on the board: fbtft drives the panel over SPI with
deferred IO, and fbcon writes a dirty rectangle per character cell. A 4x6
cell is 8 bytes of RGB565, so the per-write overhead is large relative to
the payload and the console may scroll visibly slowly. That is a property of
this driver and this panel, not of the console, and no amount of config
changes it.

diff --git a/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/esp32s3.dtsi b/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/esp32s3.dtsi
index a1f9639aa7ec1..b6c1d0e4f2a55 100644
--- a/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/esp32s3.dtsi
+++ b/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/esp32s3.dtsi
@@ -7,7 +7,7 @@ / {
 	interrupt-parent = <&pic>;

 	chosen {
-		bootargs = "earlycon=esp32s3uart,mmio32,0x60000000,115200n8,40000000 console=ttyS0,115200n8 rw root=mtd:rootfs rootfstype=cramfs panic=10 panic_console_replay ";
+		bootargs = "earlycon=esp32s3uart,mmio32,0x60000000,115200n8,40000000 console=tty1 console=ttyS0,115200n8 fbcon=font:ProFont6x11 rw root=mtd:rootfs rootfstype=cramfs panic=10 panic_console_replay no_hash_pointers memmap=64K$0x3fccc700 ";
 	};

 	aliases {
--
